// The vocabulary the other seven modules speak: who the person is, on what terms they are employed,
// what they are paid, and which state of employment they are in.
//
// The file is arranged by *what makes a value change*, which is the first discipline this example is
// written under. A single Employee record holding thirty columns is what an HR database looks like and
// what an HR domain must not: a name changes when somebody marries and a notification goes to the
// office, working hours change when a contract is rewritten, and pay changes at a pay review — three
// unrelated events that a single record makes look like one. So the person is four data with four
// reasons to change, and the employment states are a sum over them.
//
// What is on show:
//   - Data split by reason for change. PersonalIdentity never changes, PersonalName changes with a
//     notification behind it, EmploymentTerms changes when the contract does, MonthlyRemuneration when
//     pay is reviewed. The employment states spread all four.
//   - Remuneration split into the fixed part and the variable part, because the occasional revision of
//     a standard monthly remuneration (example.socialinsurance) is triggered by a change in the *fixed*
//     wage and by nothing else. A single "monthly pay" field would make that rule unstatable.
//   - The separation reason as a nested sum, read at two depths downstream: the benefit rules read the
//     outer level, since every employer cause is treated alike, and the separation certificate reads the
//     leaf, since each kind carries its own reason code.
//   - The Japanese attained-age rule. A person attains an age on the day *before* their birthday
//     (年齢計算ニ関スル法律), which decides the month long-term-care premiums start and the day pension
//     coverage ends. It is one line here and wrong in a great many payroll systems.
//   - A check digit as a behavior rather than an invariant. My Number's twelfth digit is a mod-11 fold,
//     and an invariant may not fold (an invariant may not recurse), so the format is the invariant and
//     the checksum is a behavior — the same division example.inventory draws for EAN-13.
module example.employee exposing (
    EmployeeId,
    OfficeId,
    PostalCode,
    PrefectureCode,
    PersonName,
    KanaName,
    MyNumber,
    Yen,
    toYen,
    WeeklyScheduledHours,
    Sex,
    Male,
    Female,
    Office,
    PersonalIdentity,
    PersonalName,
    Address,
    Residence,
    EmploymentType,
    FullTime,
    ContractEmployee,
    PartTime,
    Executive,
    ContractTerm,
    Indefinite,
    TermUntil,
    StudentStatus,
    DaytimeStudent,
    NotDaytimeStudent,
    EmploymentTerms,
    MonthlyRemuneration,
    LeaveKind,
    SickLeave,
    MaternityLeave,
    ChildcareLeave,
    CaregiverLeave,
    RenewalIntent,
    RenewalSought,
    RenewalNotSought,
    VoluntaryResignation,
    ContractExpiry,
    MandatoryRetirement,
    Dismissal,
    BusinessClosure,
    EncouragedResignation,
    EmployerCause,
    DismissalForMisconduct,
    Death,
    SeparationReason,
    EmploymentCommon,
    EmployedCommon,
    Prospective,
    Employed,
    OnLeave,
    Separated,
    Employee,
    ActiveEmployee,
    totalRemuneration,
    fixedRemuneration,
    scheduledWage,
    ageOn,
    attainedAgeOn,
    hire,
    beginLeave,
    LeaveBeforeHire,
    returnFromLeave,
    ReturnBeforeLeave,
    separate,
    SeparationBeforeHire,
    verifyMyNumber,
    MyNumberVerified,
    CheckDigitMismatch
)

// === Identity ===

// The company's own employee number. Six digits is what a mid-sized payroll runs on, and the format is
// an invariant rather than a convention because every one of the eight modules keys on it.
data EmployeeId = String
    invariant String.matches("[0-9]{6}", value)

// An office is an office of *application* — the unit the social-insurance system registers, not a
// building. Two offices of one company file separately and may sit in different prefectures.
data OfficeId = String
    invariant String.matches("[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{6}", value)

data PostalCode = String
    invariant String.matches("[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}", value)

// Why: the health-insurance premium rate of the 協会けんぽ is set per prefecture, so which prefecture an
// office sits in is a rate input and not an address detail. The JIS X 0401 code runs 01 to 47.
data PrefectureCode = String
    invariant String.matches("0[1-9]|[1-3][0-9]|4[0-7]", value)

data PersonName = String
    invariant String.length(value) >= 1

// Why: every social-insurance notification is filed under the katakana reading, not the kanji, because
// that is what the authority's index is keyed on. A name with no reading cannot be filed, so the
// reading is a required field and its script is an invariant.
data KanaName = String
    invariant String.matches("[ｦ-ﾟァ-ヴー 　]+", value)

// Why: a My Number is twelve digits, the twelfth being a check digit. Only the *length and script* are
// an invariant; the check digit is a fold and an invariant may not fold, so verifyMyNumber below is
// where that rule lives.
data MyNumber = String
    invariant String.matches("[0-9]{12}", value)

data Sex = Male | Female

// Money, for the whole project. Yen has no fraction, so it is an Int and never a Decimal — the only
// place a rate turns into yen is where a rounding mode is named, and that is inside the module that
// owns the rate. A negative amount is not money: a shortfall and a refund are directions, carried by
// the case that means them.
data Yen = Int
    invariant value >= 0

// Why: an amount computed at a rate is settled to the yen by dropping 50 sen and carrying anything
// above it (通貨の単位及び貨幣の発行等に関する法律第3条), which is exactly HALF_DOWN. Both insurance
// modules deduct at a rate and both settle it this way, so the rule is written once here rather than
// twice under two comments that could drift apart.
let toYen (amount: Decimal): Yen = Yen(Decimal.toInt(HALF_DOWN, amount))

// Why: the social-insurance and employment-insurance coverage tests are both stated in hours per week,
// and a week of scheduled hours is a contract term rather than a measurement. 37.5 is an ordinary
// value, so it is a Decimal.
data WeeklyScheduledHours = Decimal
    invariant value >= 0.0m && value <= 168.0m

// The registered office. The insured count is here because two rules read it: the specified-office test
// for short-time workers (example.socialinsurance) counts pension-insured heads, and the prefecture
// decides the health rate.
data Office =
    { id: OfficeId
    , prefecture: PrefectureCode
    , insuredCount: Int
    }
    invariant insuredCount >= 0

// === The person, split by what makes it change ===

// Settled when the person joins and corrected only when it was wrong. The birthday is the input of
// every age rule downstream, and the My Number is what the filing module puts on a notification.
data PersonalIdentity =
    { id: EmployeeId
    , birthday: Date
    , sex: Sex
    , myNumber: MyNumber
    }

// Changes when somebody marries, and a change here is itself a notification (氏名変更届) rather than an
// edit. That is the reason it is not part of PersonalIdentity: the two have different events behind them.
data PersonalName =
    { name: PersonName
    , kana: KanaName
    }

data Address =
    { postalCode: PostalCode
    , prefecture: PrefectureCode
    , line: String
    }
    invariant String.length(line) >= 1

// Changes when somebody moves, which is again its own notification (住所変更届).
data Residence =
    { address: Address
    }

// === The terms, which change when the contract does ===

// Why: an executive is not a worker under the Labour Standards Act, so no working-hours rule and no
// employment insurance reaches one — while health and pension insurance do. Keeping the four apart is
// what lets those two modules disagree about the same person without either writing an exception.
data EmploymentType = FullTime | ContractEmployee | PartTime | Executive

data TermUntil = { endsOn: Date }
data ContractTerm = Indefinite | TermUntil

// Why: a daytime student is excluded from employment insurance however many hours they work
// (雇用保険法第6条). It is a fact about the person's schooling and not about the contract, but it is
// read only by the coverage test, so it sits beside the hours that test also reads.
data StudentStatus = DaytimeStudent | NotDaytimeStudent

data EmploymentTerms =
    { officeId: OfficeId
    , employmentType: EmploymentType
    , weeklyHours: WeeklyScheduledHours
    , contract: ContractTerm
    , student: StudentStatus
    }

// === What is paid, which changes at a pay review ===

// Why the split: four rules read this money four different ways, and no two of them add up the same
// fields. A grade is decided from everything paid in the month; an occasional revision is triggered by
// the fixed part; the short-time coverage test reads the scheduled wage; and an overtime uplift is
// computed on a base the statute strips family, housing and commuting allowances out of. A single
// monthlyPay column leaves three of the four with nothing to read.
data MonthlyRemuneration =
    { baseSalary: Yen             // 基本給
    , scheduledAllowances: Yen    // 役職手当など、割増賃金の基礎に算入される固定的手当
    , commutingAllowance: Yen     // 通勤手当。標準報酬月額に算入し、割増賃金の基礎からは除外され、
                                  // 所得税は月15万円まで非課税。三つの規則が別々に読む
    , excludedAllowances: Yen     // 家族手当・住宅手当。割増賃金の基礎からも短時間労働者の
                                  // 8.8 万円要件からも除かれる（労働基準法第37条第5項）
    , variableAllowances: Yen     // 残業手当など、月ごとに動くもの
    }

// 所定内賃金. Read twice: by the short-time-worker wage test of example.socialinsurance, and as the base
// an overtime uplift is computed on (labour-standards §37(5) strips exactly the two fields below it).
let scheduledWage (r: MonthlyRemuneration): Yen = r.baseSalary + r.scheduledAllowances

// 固定的賃金. What the occasional-revision trigger reads: everything paid at a rate that does not move
// with the month, commuting allowance included — a commuting allowance that changes because somebody
// moved house is a fixed-wage change and does start a revision.
let fixedRemuneration (r: MonthlyRemuneration): Yen =
    scheduledWage(r) + r.commutingAllowance + r.excludedAllowances

// 報酬月額. What a grade is decided from: everything paid in the month.
let totalRemuneration (r: MonthlyRemuneration): Yen = fixedRemuneration(r) + r.variableAllowances

// === Leave and separation ===

// Why: maternity leave and childcare leave exempt both the employee's and the employer's social-insurance
// premiums (健康保険法第159条, 厚生年金保険法第81条の2); sick leave and caregiver leave do not. The
// premium module reads this sum to decide, so the four kinds are four cases rather than a leave flag
// with a reason string beside it.
data SickLeave
data MaternityLeave
data ChildcareLeave
data CaregiverLeave
data LeaveKind = SickLeave | MaternityLeave | ChildcareLeave | CaregiverLeave

// Why: a fixed-term contract that ends after the employee asked for a renewal makes them a specified-
// reason recipient, and one that ends after they did not is an ordinary expiry. The difference decides
// whether unemployment benefit is restricted, so it is a case and not a note.
data RenewalIntent = RenewalSought | RenewalNotSought

data ContractExpiry = { renewal: RenewalIntent }
data EmployerCause = Dismissal | BusinessClosure | EncouragedResignation

// The separation reason nests, and it earns the nesting because two rules read it at two depths.
// example.employmentinsurance's benefit rules read the outer level — every employer cause makes a
// specified-eligible recipient and none of them is restricted, whichever kind it is — while
// example.filing reads the leaf, because the separation certificate carries a different reason code for
// a dismissal, a closure and an encouraged resignation.
//
// Why a dismissal for serious misconduct is not under EmployerCause: it is legally a separation for a
// grave reason attributable to the worker (雇用保険法第33条第1項), which is the opposite end of the
// benefit rules from the rest of that group. Putting it inside would make "an employer cause is never
// restricted" false, and the outer level would stop being readable on its own.
data SeparationReason =
    VoluntaryResignation
  | ContractExpiry
  | MandatoryRetirement
  | EmployerCause
  | DismissalForMisconduct
  | Death

// === The states of employment ===

data EmploymentCommon =
    { identity: PersonalIdentity
    , personalName: PersonalName
    , residence: Residence
    , terms: EmploymentTerms
    , remuneration: MonthlyRemuneration
    }

data EmployedCommon =
    { ...EmploymentCommon
    , hiredOn: Date
    }

data Prospective = { ...EmploymentCommon, hireOn: Date }
data Employed    = { ...EmployedCommon }
data OnLeave     = { ...EmployedCommon, leave: LeaveKind, leaveFrom: Date }
    invariant Date.daysBetween(hiredOn, leaveFrom) >= 0
data Separated   = { ...EmployedCommon, separatedOn: Date, reason: SeparationReason }
    invariant Date.daysBetween(hiredOn, separatedOn) >= 0

data Employee = Prospective | Employed | OnLeave | Separated

// Somebody on leave is still employed: they are still insured, still accrue service, and may still
// resign. The operations that hold for both are written against this sum rather than against Employee,
// so a behavior cannot be handed somebody who has not started or has already left.
data ActiveEmployee = Employed | OnLeave

// === Age, and the day it is attained ===

// The ordinary calendar age, which is what a tax rule means when it says "under 19" or "70 or over".
let ageOn (birthday: Date, on: Date): Int =
    Date.year(on) - Date.year(birthday)
        - (if Date.month(on) * 100 + Date.day(on) < Date.month(birthday) * 100 + Date.day(birthday)
           then 1 else 0)

// Why: under 年齢計算ニ関スル法律 a person attains an age on the day *before* their birthday, and the
// social-insurance system means that age everywhere — long-term-care premiums start in the month an
// employee attains 40, pension coverage ends the day they attain 70, and somebody born on the first of
// a month therefore attains it in the previous month. Shifting the birthday back one day is the whole
// of the rule, and it is what a payroll gets wrong for exactly the people born on the first.
let attainedAgeOn (birthday: Date, on: Date): Int =
    ageOn(Date.addDays(0 - 1, birthday), on)

// === The transitions ===

behavior hire : (prospect: Prospective) -> Employed
    constructs Employed

let hire (prospect) = Employed { ...prospect, hiredOn = prospect.hireOn }

behavior beginLeave : (employee: Employed, kind: LeaveKind, from: Date) -> OnLeave | LeaveBeforeHire
    constructs OnLeave

let beginLeave (employee, kind, from) = {
    // The guard discharges OnLeave's invariant, which would otherwise abort — and an abort is not
    // something a boundary can tell anybody about. A leave dated before the hire is a data-entry error
    // the system does report.
    guard Date.daysBetween(employee.hiredOn, from) >= 0 else LeaveBeforeHire
    OnLeave { ...employee, leave = kind, leaveFrom = from }
}

behavior returnFromLeave : (employee: OnLeave, on: Date) -> Employed | ReturnBeforeLeave
    constructs Employed

let returnFromLeave (employee, on) = {
    guard Date.daysBetween(employee.leaveFrom, on) >= 0 else ReturnBeforeLeave
    Employed { ...employee }
}

// Resigning while on leave is ordinary, so the input is the sum of both active states rather than
// Employed alone. A prospective hire has nothing to resign from and somebody already separated cannot
// separate twice; neither call typechecks, and no guard says so.
//
// The guard reads `employee.hiredOn` off the sum and the construction spreads the same shared part, so
// what the behavior states is the rule and nothing about which active state the employee was in.
behavior separate : (employee: ActiveEmployee, on: Date, reason: SeparationReason)
        -> Separated | SeparationBeforeHire
    constructs Separated

let separate (employee, on, reason) = {
    guard Date.daysBetween(employee.hiredOn, on) >= 0 else SeparationBeforeHire
    Separated { ...employee, separatedOn = on, reason = reason }
}

// === The My Number check digit ===
// Why: the twelfth digit is 11 − (Σ P_n × Q_n mod 11), taken as 0 when that remainder is 0 or 1, where
// Q_n is the n-th digit counted from the eleventh and P_n is n+1 for n up to 6 and n−5 after that
// (行政手続における特定の個人を識別するための番号の利用等に関する法律施行令第8条). A transposed pair of
// digits is what it catches, which is the mistake a person typing a number from a card actually makes.

// A digit's value is its code point less that of "0", read from the string rather than written as 48.
let digitValue (c: Int): Int =
    match List.get(0, String.codePoints("0")) with
        | Some zero -> c - zero
        | None -> unreachable "a one-character string has a first code point"

let weightedSum (body: List<Int>): Int =
    List.sum(List.mapIndexed((i, c) -> (if i <= 5 then i + 2 else i - 4) * digitValue(c),
                             List.reverse(body)))

let expectedCheckDigit (body: List<Int>): Int = {
    let r = Int.floorMod(weightedSum(body), 11)
    if r <= 1 then 0 else 11 - r
}

behavior verifyMyNumber : (number: MyNumber) -> MyNumberVerified | CheckDigitMismatch

let verifyMyNumber (number) = {
    let codes = String.codePoints(number.value)
    let body = List.take(11, codes)
    let check = List.drop(11, codes)
    // The invariant proved there are twelve digits, so the twelfth is there; List.get still answers an
    // optional, and the absent arm is the one the type has already ruled out.
    match List.get(0, check) with
        | Some c -> if digitValue(c) == expectedCheckDigit(body)
                    then MyNumberVerified
                    else CheckDigitMismatch
        | None   -> unreachable "the invariant proved twelve digits, so the twelfth is there"
}

example verifyMyNumber
    | "a number whose check digit is the one the fold computes verifies" :
        (MyNumber("123456789018")) -> MyNumberVerified
    | "transposing two digits changes the weighted sum, so the check digit no longer agrees" :
        (MyNumber("123456789081")) -> CheckDigitMismatch
    | "a twelve-digit number whose weighted sum leaves a remainder of one has the check digit 0" :
        (MyNumber("123456789000")) -> MyNumberVerified
    | "a remainder of zero is also a check digit of 0" :
        (MyNumber("000000000000")) -> MyNumberVerified
    | "a remainder of one with any other twelfth digit does not verify" :
        (MyNumber("123456789005")) -> CheckDigitMismatch

// === The people the rows are about ===

let satoIdentity = PersonalIdentity
    { id = EmployeeId("000123")
    , birthday = Date("1990-04-01")
    , sex = Female
    , myNumber = MyNumber("123456789018")
    }

let satoTerms = EmploymentTerms
    { officeId = OfficeId("13-000001")
    , employmentType = FullTime
    , weeklyHours = WeeklyScheduledHours(40.0m)
    , contract = Indefinite
    , student = NotDaytimeStudent
    }

let satoPay = MonthlyRemuneration
    { baseSalary = Yen(260000)
    , scheduledAllowances = Yen(20000)
    , commutingAllowance = Yen(15000)
    , excludedAllowances = Yen(5000)
    , variableAllowances = Yen(0)
    }

let satoCommon = EmploymentCommon
    { identity = satoIdentity
    , personalName = PersonalName { name = PersonName("Sato Hanako"), kana = KanaName("ｻﾄｳ ﾊﾅｺ") }
    , residence = Residence
        { address = Address { postalCode = PostalCode("100-0001"), prefecture = PrefectureCode("13"), line = "Chiyoda 1-1" } }
    , terms = satoTerms
    , remuneration = satoPay
    }

// A man on a fixed-term contract who is still a daytime student, with a one-character name and address.
let taroCommon = EmploymentCommon
    { identity = PersonalIdentity { ...satoIdentity, id = EmployeeId("000456"), sex = Male }
    , personalName = PersonalName { name = PersonName("T"), kana = KanaName("ﾀ") }
    , residence = Residence
        { address = Address { postalCode = PostalCode("530-0001"), prefecture = PrefectureCode("27"), line = "x" } }
    , terms = EmploymentTerms
        { ...satoTerms
        , officeId = OfficeId("27-000001")
        , employmentType = ContractEmployee
        , weeklyHours = WeeklyScheduledHours(30.0m)
        , contract = TermUntil { endsOn = Date("2027-03-31") }
        , student = DaytimeStudent
        }
    , remuneration = MonthlyRemuneration { ...satoPay, baseSalary = Yen(200000) }
    }

// A part-timer on a contract that schedules no hours at all.
let partTimeCommon = EmploymentCommon
    { ...satoCommon
    , terms = EmploymentTerms { ...satoTerms, employmentType = PartTime, weeklyHours = WeeklyScheduledHours(0.0m) }
    , remuneration = MonthlyRemuneration { ...satoPay, baseSalary = Yen(110000) }
    }

// An executive, whose hours are not scheduled by the week and so run to the end of it.
let executiveCommon = EmploymentCommon
    { ...satoCommon
    , terms = EmploymentTerms { ...satoTerms, employmentType = Executive, weeklyHours = WeeklyScheduledHours(168.0m) }
    , remuneration = MonthlyRemuneration { ...satoPay, baseSalary = Yen(800000) }
    }

let satoProspective = Prospective { ...satoCommon, hireOn = Date("2026-04-01") }
let satoEmployed = Employed { ...satoCommon, hiredOn = Date("2026-04-01") }
let satoOnLeave = OnLeave { ...satoEmployed, leave = MaternityLeave, leaveFrom = Date("2026-09-01") }

let taroEmployed = Employed { ...taroCommon, hiredOn = Date("2025-10-01") }
let partTimeEmployed = Employed { ...partTimeCommon, hiredOn = Date("2024-04-01") }
let executiveEmployed = Employed { ...executiveCommon, hiredOn = Date("2020-04-01") }

// On leave from the day of the hire, which is the earliest the invariant allows.
let taroOnLeave = OnLeave { ...taroEmployed, leave = SickLeave, leaveFrom = Date("2025-10-01") }
let partTimeOnLeave = OnLeave { ...partTimeEmployed, leave = ChildcareLeave, leaveFrom = Date("2025-06-01") }
let executiveOnLeave = OnLeave { ...executiveEmployed, leave = CaregiverLeave, leaveFrom = Date("2026-01-15") }

example hire
    | "a prospective hire becomes an employee hired on the day the offer says" :
        (satoProspective) -> satoEmployed
    | "a man on a fixed-term contract keeps every term of it when he is hired" :
        (Prospective { ...taroCommon, hireOn = Date("2025-10-01") }) -> taroEmployed
    | "a part-timer is hired on the terms the offer carried" :
        (Prospective { ...partTimeCommon, hireOn = Date("2024-04-01") }) -> partTimeEmployed
    | "an executive is hired on the terms the offer carried" :
        (Prospective { ...executiveCommon, hireOn = Date("2020-04-01") }) -> executiveEmployed

example beginLeave
    | "a leave that starts after the hire is recorded with its kind and start" :
        (satoEmployed, MaternityLeave, Date("2026-09-01")) -> satoOnLeave
    | "a leave that starts on the hire day is not before it" :
        (taroEmployed, SickLeave, Date("2025-10-01")) -> taroOnLeave
    | "a part-timer may take childcare leave" :
        (partTimeEmployed, ChildcareLeave, Date("2025-06-01")) -> partTimeOnLeave
    | "an executive may take caregiver leave" :
        (executiveEmployed, CaregiverLeave, Date("2026-01-15")) -> executiveOnLeave
    | "a leave dated the day before the hire is a data-entry error the system reports" :
        (satoEmployed, ChildcareLeave, Date("2026-03-31")) -> LeaveBeforeHire
    | "a leave dated years before the hire is reported the same way" :
        (taroEmployed, SickLeave, Date("2019-01-01")) -> LeaveBeforeHire

example returnFromLeave
    | "returning after the leave began gives back an employee hired on the same day" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2027-03-01")) -> satoEmployed
    | "returning on the day the leave began is not before it" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2026-09-01")) -> satoEmployed
    | "an employee on sick leave from the day of the hire returns to the same terms" :
        (taroOnLeave, Date("2025-12-01")) -> taroEmployed
    | "a part-timer returns from childcare leave" :
        (partTimeOnLeave, Date("2026-06-01")) -> partTimeEmployed
    | "an executive returns from caregiver leave" :
        (executiveOnLeave, Date("2026-03-01")) -> executiveEmployed
    | "returning the day before the leave began is an error the system reports" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2026-08-31")) -> ReturnBeforeLeave
    | "returning years before the leave began is reported the same way" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2020-01-01")) -> ReturnBeforeLeave

example separate
    | "an employee at work who retires is separated on that day for that reason" :
        (satoEmployed, Date("2027-03-31"), MandatoryRetirement)
        -> Separated { ...satoEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2027-03-31"), reason = MandatoryRetirement }
    | "somebody on leave may still resign" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2026-12-31"), VoluntaryResignation)
        -> Separated { ...satoEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-12-31"), reason = VoluntaryResignation }
    | "separating on the hire day is not before it" :
        (satoEmployed, Date("2026-04-01"), Death)
        -> Separated { ...satoEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-04-01"), reason = Death }
    | "a fixed-term contract that ends after renewal was sought carries that in the reason" :
        (taroEmployed, Date("2027-03-31"), ContractExpiry { renewal = RenewalSought })
        -> Separated { ...taroEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2027-03-31"), reason = ContractExpiry { renewal = RenewalSought } }
    | "one that ends with no renewal sought says so" :
        (taroOnLeave, Date("2027-03-31"), ContractExpiry { renewal = RenewalNotSought })
        -> Separated { ...taroEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2027-03-31"), reason = ContractExpiry { renewal = RenewalNotSought } }
    | "a business closure separates a part-timer" :
        (partTimeEmployed, Date("2026-09-30"), BusinessClosure)
        -> Separated { ...partTimeEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-09-30"), reason = BusinessClosure }
    | "an encouraged resignation separates a part-timer on leave" :
        (partTimeOnLeave, Date("2026-02-28"), EncouragedResignation)
        -> Separated { ...partTimeEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-02-28"), reason = EncouragedResignation }
    | "a dismissal for misconduct separates an executive" :
        (executiveEmployed, Date("2026-05-31"), DismissalForMisconduct)
        -> Separated { ...executiveEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-05-31"), reason = DismissalForMisconduct }
    | "an executive on leave may be dismissed" :
        (executiveOnLeave, Date("2026-02-28"), Dismissal)
        -> Separated { ...executiveEmployed, separatedOn = Date("2026-02-28"), reason = Dismissal }
    | "separating the day before the hire is an error the system reports" :
        (satoEmployed, Date("2026-03-31"), Dismissal) -> SeparationBeforeHire
    | "separating years before the hire is reported the same way" :
        (satoOnLeave, Date("2019-01-01"), Death) -> SeparationBeforeHire
